          "text": "Correct: I omitted the cryptographic consequence. **Unlink must revoke the node’s enrollment**, not merely stop synchronization.\n\nWe should also revise the identity model slightly:\n\n- `node_id` is stable provenance for the local installation.\n- Each hub link creates a separate `enrollment_id` and cryptographic keypair/credential.\n- Keys are revocable and rotatable; `node_id` is not an immortal public key.\n\n## Unlink transaction\n\n1. Pause new sync, remote inference, and routed Runs.\n2. Hub revokes the `enrollment_id`, public key, and issued credentials.\n3. Hub returns a durable revocation receipt.\n4. Local node destroys its enrollment private key and tokens.\n5. Local data remains untouched.\n6. Local node retains non-secret link history: hub identity, account fingerprint, enrollment ID, and revocation receipt.\n\nIf the hub is unreachable, complete remote revocation is physically impossible. The UI must say **“Disconnected locally; remote revocation pending”**, not “Unlinked.” The user can complete revocation when connectivity returns or from the hub’s account UI.\n\n## Different-account relinking\n\nThere should still be no local per-record `user_id`. What remains after unlink is a `last_linked_account_fingerprint`, specifically to distinguish these cases:\n\n| State | Behavior |\n| --- | --- |\n| Never linked, or empty local store | Ordinary first-link prompt. |\n| Previously linked to the same account | Fresh OAuth/re-verification, new enrollment key, then normal bidirectional reconciliation. |\n| Previously linked to another account, populated store | Do not use the ordinary link flow. This is cross-account data transfer. |\n\nMultiple generic “Are you really sure?” dialogs are confirmation theater. The safer UX is one explicit, semantic decision:\n\n```text\nThis node contains data previously synchronized with Account A.\n\nLinking this existing profile to Account B would copy its Chats,\nKnowledge Spaces, memory, and configuration to Account B.\nAccount A will retain its existing copy.\n\nRecommended: create a separate empty profile for Account B.\n\n[Create separate profile]\n[Transfer this profile to Account B…]\n[Cancel]\n```\n\nThe transfer path should require:\n\n- fresh authentication to Account B;\n- local device re-verification;\n- an inventory of what will be copied;\n- explicit disclosure that Account A retains its copy;\n- typing the destination account identifier or equivalent semantic confirmation; and\n- a new enrollment identity scoped to Account B.\n\nThere is also an unresolved ID problem: copying Account A’s resources into Account B may not be compatible with globally stable resource IDs. Therefore cross-account transfer should be a separate import/ownership-transfer workflow, not ordinary sync. For the first version, I would support only:\n\n1. Relink the populated profile to the same account.\n2. Create a new empty profile for a different account.\n\nDefer cross-account corpus transfer until its identity and provenance semantics are designed.\n\nDo you agree with blocking ordinary different-account relinking for populated profiles and routing users toward a new profile or a future explicit transfer workflow?",
